A provider is a plugin that maps Terraform resource types to real cloud API calls. The AWS provider translates `aws_instance` into EC2 CreateInstance API calls. Providers are versioned and downloaded from the Terraform Registry.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"  # pin to avoid breaking changes
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}
```

A resource represents a single infrastructure object: an EC2 instance, S3 bucket, RDS database, IAM role. Each resource has a type (from the provider) and a local name.

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "app_server" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"
  tags = {
    Name = "app-server"
  }
}
```

Resource attributes can reference other resources: `aws_instance.app_server.id`.

# Remote state backend: S3 + DynamoDB for AWS
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state-prod"
    key            = "services/payment-api/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    encrypt        = true            # AES-256 encryption at rest
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-state-lock"  # locking — prevents concurrent apply
  }
}

# DynamoDB table for locking (created separately, once per account)
# resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "terraform_locks" {
#   name         = "terraform-state-lock"
#   billing_mode = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
#   hash_key     = "LockID"
#   attribute {
#     name = "LockID"
#     type = "S"
#   }
# }

# Sharing state between configurations
data "terraform_remote_state" "network" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "my-terraform-state-prod"
    key    = "network/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

# Use output from the network stack
resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  subnet_id = data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.private_subnet_id
}

When `terraform apply` fails mid-way — network timeout, API throttling, or a resource creation error — Terraform writes a partial state. Some resources were created, others were not. This is not a corrupted state; Terraform accurately recorded what it managed to create. Re-running `apply` with the same plan will attempt to create the remaining resources and update the ones that already exist.

The `-target` flag lets you apply changes to a single resource, which is tempting during recovery but dangerous in regular use. Targeting skips dependency graph evaluation, meaning Terraform will not create prerequisites or update dependents. Use it only for emergency recovery — never in CI pipelines.

Lifecycle rules provide guardrails against accidental destruction. `prevent_destroy = true` on a resource block causes Terraform to error if any plan attempts to destroy that resource. This is essential for databases, S3 buckets with production data, and encryption keys. The `create_before_destroy` lifecycle rule ensures a replacement resource is created before the old one is destroyed, reducing downtime for stateless resources like launch templates.

```hcl
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  # ... configuration
  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

resource "aws_launch_template" "app" {
  # ... configuration
  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}
```

For disaster recovery, S3 versioning on the state bucket is non-negotiable. If state is corrupted beyond repair, restore a previous version from the S3 version history. Pair this with a periodic `terraform plan` in CI that detects drift — resources changed outside Terraform — before it accumulates into a large, risky remediation apply.

# Module structure: modules/spring-app/
# ├── variables.tf   — inputs
# ├── main.tf        — resources
# └── outputs.tf     — outputs

# variables.tf
variable "app_name" {
  description = "Name of the Spring Boot application"
  type        = string
}
variable "instance_count" {
  description = "Number of ECS tasks to run"
  type        = number
  default     = 2
}
variable "docker_image" {
  description = "Docker image URI (ECR)"
  type        = string
}

# main.tf — creates ECS service, task definition, ALB target group
resource "aws_ecs_service" "app" {
  name            = var.app_name
  desired_count   = var.instance_count
  task_definition = aws_ecs_task_definition.app.arn
  # ... other config
}

# outputs.tf
output "service_url" {
  description = "URL of the deployed application"
  value       = "https://${aws_lb.app.dns_name}"
}

# Calling the module from root configuration
module "payment_service" {
  source         = "./modules/spring-app"
  app_name       = "payment-service"
  instance_count = 3
  docker_image   = "123456789.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/payment:v1.2.3"
}

# Reference module output
output "payment_url" {
  value = module.payment_service.service_url
}

# variables.tf — declare with type and description
variable "environment" {
  description = "Deployment environment (dev, staging, prod)"
  type        = string
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "staging", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "Environment must be dev, staging, or prod."
  }
}
variable "instance_count" {
  type    = number
  default = 1
}
variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true  # redacts from plan output and logs
}

# dev.tfvars
environment    = "dev"
instance_count = 1

# prod.tfvars
environment    = "prod"
instance_count = 5

# Apply with environment-specific values
# terraform apply -var-file=prod.tfvars

# Environment variable override (highest precedence after -var flag)
# export TF_VAR_db_password="secret123"

# Workspaces — separate state per workspace
# terraform workspace new staging
# terraform workspace select prod
# terraform workspace list

# Use workspace name in config
resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  count = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? 5 : 1
  tags  = { Environment = terraform.workspace }
}

Beyond simple strings and numbers, Terraform variables support complex types that enable powerful parameterization patterns. Maps and objects let you define structured configuration per environment or per service in a single variable.

```hcl
variable "service_config" {
  type = map(object({
    instance_count = number
    instance_type  = string
    enable_cdn     = bool
  }))
  default = {
    payment = { instance_count = 3, instance_type = "t3.large", enable_cdn = false }
    catalog = { instance_count = 2, instance_type = "t3.medium", enable_cdn = true }
  }
}

resource "aws_ecs_service" "app" {
  for_each      = var.service_config
  name          = each.key
  desired_count = each.value.instance_count
}
```

`for_each` with map variables is the idiomatic way to create multiple similar resources without `count` index fragility. With `count`, removing the second item re-indexes everything after it, forcing unnecessary replacements. With `for_each`, each resource is keyed by map key — removing `catalog` only affects that resource.

`locals` blocks compute intermediate values that do not belong in the variable interface. Use them for tag merging, naming conventions, and conditional logic that callers should not control.

```hcl
locals {
  common_tags = merge(var.extra_tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    ManagedBy   = "terraform"
    Team        = var.team_name
  })
  name_prefix = "${var.project}-${var.environment}"
}
```

This pattern ensures every resource in the configuration inherits consistent tags for cost allocation and on-call routing, regardless of which `-var-file` was used during apply.

A production Spring Boot deployment needs autoscaling, centralized logging, and properly configured health checks beyond the basic infrastructure.

ECS autoscaling uses Application Auto Scaling with CloudWatch metrics. Configure a target-tracking policy based on CPU or custom metrics from Spring Actuator.

```hcl
resource "aws_appautoscaling_target" "ecs" {
  max_capacity       = 10
  min_capacity       = 2
  resource_id        = "service/${aws_ecs_cluster.main.name}/${aws_ecs_service.app.name}"
  scalable_dimension = "ecs:service:DesiredCount"
  service_namespace  = "ecs"
}

resource "aws_appautoscaling_policy" "cpu" {
  name               = "cpu-auto-scaling"
  resource_id        = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs.resource_id
  scalable_dimension = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs.scalable_dimension
  service_namespace  = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs.service_namespace
  policy_type        = "TargetTrackingScaling"
  target_tracking_scaling_policy_configuration {
    predefined_metric_specification {
      predefined_metric_type = "ECSServiceAverageCPUUtilization"
    }
    target_value = 70.0
  }
}
```

For logging, Terraform should create a CloudWatch Log Group with a retention policy. The ECS task definition references it in the `logConfiguration` block using the `awslogs` driver. Set `retention_in_days` to 30 or 90 to control costs — unbounded log retention in CloudWatch is a common billing surprise.

The ALB health check should point to Spring Boot's `/actuator/health` endpoint with an appropriate interval and threshold. A `healthy_threshold` of 2 and `unhealthy_threshold` of 3 with a 30-second interval provides a reasonable balance between responsiveness and stability. Set the health check `matcher` to `200` and ensure the Spring Actuator health endpoint does not include slow downstream checks (like database connectivity) that could cause false negatives during transient network blips. Use the Actuator's health groups feature to expose a lightweight liveness check at `/actuator/health/liveness` for the ALB and reserve the full check for readiness.

# 1. Look up the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI (managed by AWS, not Terraform)
data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux" {
  most_recent = true
  owners      = ["amazon"]
  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["amzn2-ami-hvm-*-x86_64-gp2"]
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id  # always up-to-date AMI
  instance_type = "t3.medium"
}

# 2. Reference an existing VPC created by another team
data "aws_vpc" "existing" {
  tags = { Name = "shared-production-vpc" }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "app" {
  vpc_id     = data.aws_vpc.existing.id
  cidr_block = "10.0.10.0/24"
}

# 3. Get current AWS account ID (no hard-coding)
data "aws_caller_identity" "current" {}

resource "aws_iam_role" "app" {
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Statement = [{
      Principal = { Service = "ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com" }
      Effect = "Allow"
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

# 4. Read a secret from Secrets Manager
data "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db_password" {
  secret_id = "prod/db/password"
}

locals {
  db_password = data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db_password.secret_string
}

Data sources have subtle behaviors that catch even experienced Terraform users.

Data sources are evaluated during `plan`, not `apply`. This means the queried resource must already exist before you run `terraform plan`. If you create a VPC in one configuration and reference it via a data source in another, the VPC must exist first. This creates an implicit ordering dependency between configurations that is not enforced by Terraform — it is your responsibility to apply them in the correct order.

A common pitfall is using data sources for resources that might not exist yet. For example, looking up an ACM certificate by domain name before the certificate has been validated. The plan will fail with a "no matching certificate found" error. The solution is either to create the certificate in the same configuration (making it a resource, not a data source) or to use a `depends_on` on the data source pointing to the creation step if the certificate is created in an earlier module.

Data sources re-read on every plan. Unlike resources that cache state, data sources query the live API each time. For `data.aws_ami`, this is desirable — you always get the latest AMI. But for expensive or rate-limited APIs, frequent planning can cause throttling. Structure your data sources so that high-cost lookups are in modules that change infrequently.

```hcl
data "aws_availability_zones" "available" {
  state = "available"
  filter {
    name   = "opt-in-status"
    values = ["opt-in-not-required"]
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "app" {
  count             = length(data.aws_availability_zones.available.names)
  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id
  cidr_block        = cidrsubnet(aws_vpc.main.cidr_block, 8, count.index)
  availability_zone = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[count.index]
}
```

This pattern dynamically creates one subnet per available AZ, making the configuration portable across regions with different AZ counts. It eliminates the need to hard-code AZ names, which differ between regions and even between AWS accounts in the same region due to AZ ID mapping.

# Layer 1: Built-in validation
# terraform fmt -check     — formatting check in CI
# terraform validate       — syntax and type checking

# Layer 2: Static analysis tools
# tflint — provider-specific best practices (e.g., invalid instance type)
# checkov -- security scanning (e.g., S3 bucket encryption, SG not open to 0.0.0.0)
# trivy --  IaC security scanning

# Layer 3: terraform test (Terraform 1.6+ native testing)
# tests/main.tftest.hcl
run "creates_vpc_with_correct_cidr" {
  command = plan  # or apply for real infra

  variables {
    cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
    name       = "test-vpc"
  }

  assert {
    condition     = aws_vpc.main.cidr_block == "10.0.0.0/16"
    error_message = "VPC CIDR block does not match input variable"
  }
}

